4
read :- user opens home timeline
write :- user posts tweet

Read Optimization (user opens home timeline)
-> timeline cache (redis)
   store each users latest timeline in redis
   reading from redis takes a few milliseconds instead of querying the database.

-> tweet cache
   timeline stores only tweet IDs.
   actual tweet content is cached separtely in Redis.

-> if cache misses occur , read from database replicas instead of the primary database
   distributes read load.

-> pagination :- return only 20-50 tweets at a time.
                 load more when the user scrolls.

-> cursor-based pagination :- instead of offset 1000
                              use after TweetID = 98765
                              much faster

-> cdn for images/videos :- tweet contains media
                            don't fetch images from twitter servers
                            server them through cdn

Write Optimization (User posts a tweet)

-> sharding tweet database : reducing latency
   store tweets across multiple database servers

-> tweet api -> store tweet -> kafka -> return success
   don't update millions of timelines synchronously.

-> fanout on write (normal users)
   if alice has 500 followers , each followers' redis timeline is updated

-> fanout on read (celebrities)
   suppose a celebrity has 100M followers.
   dont' update 100M timelines.
   instead , store tweet -> done.

   when user opens twitter -> merge celeberity tweets -> return feed.
   save millions of writes

-> batch writes
   instead of write user1 , user2 , user3 , write user4.
   batch them and write user1 , user2 , user3 

-> skip inactive users 
   don't push tweets to users who haven't opened twitter in months.
   generate their timeline only when they come back.
   this reduces unnecessary writes.








                       
                                                             
                                                              





